3 HARDWARE IMPLEMENTATION

I owe much of the implementation ideas to other processors, especially
the 2nd generation RISCs such as R4000, supersparc, PPC601 and alpha
21064.  It is because most of the design decisions were made during 
1993-1994.  I would like to incorporate good features of the 3rd
generation RISCs into STM in the future.

3-1 Pipeline

Current implementation of STM has a 7-stage pipeline.

	IA stage : Insn fetch address generation
	IB 	 : Read I-cache
	D	 : Insn dispatch/decode & register file(RF) read
	A	 : Operand adrs generation / reg-reg operation
	B	 : Read D-cache
	E	 : Reg-mem operation
	W	 : Write RF

IA and IB stages constitute the "insn pipeline"; D-W stages the
"execution pipeline".  The insn pipe and the exec pipe work async-
ronously; the insn pipe writes the insn stream to the regs called
IWQ (Insn Word Queue), and the exec pipe reads the insns from IWQ.

The exec pipe consist of three components:

	LS-pipe : handles load/store and mem-op insns
	EX-pipe : handles reg- or immed-op insns and privileged insns
	JB-pipe : handles control flow insns (branches)

STM is 2-way superscalar; two insns (if possible) are fetched from IWQ
in D stage and dispatched to the pipes corresponding to the insn.
These two insns must be of different kind (use different pipes), or
only one insn is dispatched.  Therefore, for better performance, the
compiler should issue insns so that two succsessive insns are of
different kind.  Also, the 2nd insn is not dispatched if the 1st is a
branch insn.

The functions of A, B, E and W stages are quite the same as other
processors.  The diagram below shows the data flow.  It has two ALUs;
