The two insns which are simultaneously issued will go through the pipes
in syncronism, i.e. no out-of-order execution.  The stages later than
B in EX-pipe and JB-pipe have nothing to do in particular, but these
stages are necessary because all the updates of the machine states are
made in W stage, in order to support precise interruption.  Register
renaming or reorder buffer are not necessary in this processor
structure.

Each stage has various 'tags' as well as the data.  Tags include the
register address, operation code, validity bit of the stage, etc.
These tags are passed from stage to stage, syncronously with the 
progression of the pipeline.  The tags of stage X will be latched to
stage X+1 if X does not stall nor get cancelled.  (Well, exactly speaking,
there are some exceptions... for more details, RTFS.)

All the data bypasses are done if ever possible, except for privileged
insns.  If the result of a mem-op insn M is used by a following reg-op
insn R, R can use the data in A stage when M is in W stage.  If M is 
a mere (word-)load insn, the data is available when M is in E stage.
Before the data becomes ready, R will stall in A stage.  

A reg-op insn R' and a mem-op insn M' which uses the result of R' can
be issued simultaneously without causing any interlock, because the
result will be bypassed from EX-pipe to LS-pipe.

When some data is (word-)stored to a memory location and then immediately
(word-)loaded from the same location, the data will be bypassed
from the store buffer, causing no interlock.

